// BIZCOM question bank. Written from the LMS Module 1-5 Ebooks and Study Guides only.
// Row: [module, question, options, correctIndex (0-based), reason, source, wrong-option notes]
window.QBANK = {
  subject: "Business Communication", code: "BIZCOM",
  modules: {"1":"Communication Basics","2":"Business Writing","3":"Business Writing in Action","4":"Presentation","5":"Crisis, International & Group Communication"},
  rows: [
[1,"Business communication is defined as exchanging information in order to promote an organization's goals, objectives, aims, and activities. This definition also states it increases which of the following?",["Employee satisfaction","Profits within the company","Brand awareness","Product quality"],1,"The ebook defines business communication as exchanging information to promote goals and activities, as well as increase profits within the company.","Ebook","Other options: Brand awareness and product quality are outcomes of good communication but are not part of this definition. · Employee satisfaction is a benefit, not the stated purpose in the definition. · The exact phrase in the ebook is 'increase profits within the company'."],
[1,"Effective business communication is described as a sharing process involving how many parties?",["At least one","Two or more","Exactly three","More than five"],1,"The ebook states: 'Effective business communication is a sharing process involving two or more parties sending a message that is easily understood by each person.'","Ebook","Other options: 'At least one' could include monologue — communication requires a receiver. · The definition says 'two or more', not exactly two. · There is no upper limit mentioned in the definition."],
[1,"Which of the following is listed as the FIRST of the eight ways to improve communication skills in the ebook?",["Learn to listen first","Teamwork","Stop assuming","Ask questions"],2,"The ebook lists 'Stop assuming' as the first of the eight ways to improve communication skills.","Ebook","Other options: Learn to listen first is item 3. · Teamwork is item 2. · Ask questions is item 4."],
[1,"According to the ebook, good listeners use the techniques of clarification and reflection to do what?",["Improve their vocabulary","Confirm what the other person has said and avoid confusion","Speed up the conversation","Evaluate the speaker's credibility"],1,"The ebook states good listeners use clarification and reflection 'to confirm what the other person has said and avoid any confusion.'","Ebook","Other options: Reflection is about confirmation, not vocabulary building. · Active listening slows conversations to increase accuracy. · Evaluating credibility is a critical thinking skill, not a listening technique per the ebook."],
[1,"The ebook describes Emotional Intelligence in communication as being aware of and understanding your own emotions, and using this to do what?",["Dominate conversations","Understand and work well with others","Avoid emotional topics","Make purely rational decisions"],1,"The ebook states: 'Be aware of and understand your own emotions and be able to master them, in order to understand and work well with others.'","Ebook","Other options: EI is about empathy and collaboration, not domination. · EI embraces emotions rather than avoiding them. · EI adds emotional context to decisions, not purely rational outcomes."],
[1,"Which of the following is NOT one of the five ways to deliver your message effectively as listed in the ebook?",["Be clear on the message","Prioritize","Set context and expectations","Use passive voice"],3,"The five delivery keys are: Be clear on the message, Prioritize, Set context and expectations, Appearance, Effective use of body language. Passive voice is not listed.","Ebook","Other options: All three other options are explicitly listed delivery keys. · 'Use passive voice' contradicts effective writing principles in the ebook. · Appearance is the 4th delivery key — often missed."],
[1,"The ebook states that verbal communication principles are based on which five characteristics of language?",["Language has rules; is dynamic; is universal; is visual; is permanent","Language has rules; shapes reality; is arbitrary and symbolic; is abstract; organizes reality","Language is spoken; written; formal; informal; digital","Language is simple; complex; direct; indirect; cultural"],1,"The five verbal communication principles from the ebook are: Language Has Rules, Our Reality Is Shaped by Our Language, Language Is Arbitrary and Symbolic, Language Is Abstract, Language Organizes and Classifies Reality.","Ebook","Other options: 'Universal' and 'permanent' are not listed principles. · Visual and digital relate to communication channels, not verbal principles. · Simple/complex/direct are characteristics of writing, not the listed verbal principles."],
[1,"Syntactic rules in language govern which of the following?",["The meaning of words","The structure of sentences and word order","The context of social custom","The tone and pitch of speech"],1,"The ebook states: 'Syntactic rules govern the structure of a sentence, usually including word order, in a given language.'","Ebook","Other options: Semantic rules govern meaning of words, not syntactic rules. · Contextual rules govern meaning according to context and social custom. · Tone and pitch relate to delivery, not syntactic rules."],
[1,"According to the ebook, the relationship between speech sounds and their meanings is described as:",["Logical and universal","Arbitrary and symbolic","Fixed and permanent","Cultural and biological"],1,"The ebook explicitly states: 'Language Is Arbitrary and Symbolic' — different languages use different sounds for the same meaning, confirming no inherent logical connection.","Ebook","Other options: If the relationship were logical, all languages would use the same sounds — they do not. · 'Fixed and permanent' contradicts that language changes over time. · Biological basis of language is a separate field; the ebook calls language arbitrary."],
[1,"The ebook lists ten barriers to effective communication. Which of the following is listed as a 'Psychological Barrier'?",["Background noise and poor lighting","Stage fear, speech disorders, phobia, depression","Eye sight problems and dyslexia","Cultural differences in dress and food"],1,"The ebook states: 'Psychological Barriers: Various mental and psychological issues may prove to be the barriers to effective communication. Some people have stage fear, speech disorders, phobia, depression etc.'","Ebook","Other options: Background noise and poor lighting are Physical Barriers. · Eye sight problems and dyslexia are Physiological Barriers. · Cultural differences in dress and food are Cultural Barriers."],
[1,"Which barrier to communication arises due to misinformation, lack of appropriate training, and lack of supervision?",["Attitude Barrier","Cultural Barrier","Organizational Structure Barrier","Physiological Barrier"],2,"The ebook states: 'Organizational Structure Barriers: This mostly arises because of misinformation, lack of appropriate training and/or lack of supervision.'","Ebook","Other options: Attitude Barrier relates to introversion, shyness, or egocentric behavior. · Cultural Barrier relates to differences in culture, religion, and customs. · Physiological Barrier includes physical impairments like poor eyesight or dyslexia."],
[1,"The ebook identifies four emphasis strategies to keep communications dynamic. Which of the following is NOT one of them?",["Visual Communication","Signposts","Internal Summaries and Foreshadowing","Emotional Storytelling"],3,"The four emphasis strategies listed in the ebook are: Visual Communication, Signposts, Internal Summaries and Foreshadowing, and Repetition. Emotional Storytelling is not listed.","Ebook","Other options: Visual Communication is the first emphasis strategy. · Signposts are keywords that alert audience to topic changes. · Repetition (the 4th strategy) is often confused — Emotional Storytelling is NOT a listed strategy."],
[1,"Signposts in verbal communication are described as indicators or key words that alert the audience to which of the following?",["The speaker's personal opinion","A change in topic, a tangential explanation, an example, or a conclusion","The end of the presentation only","Statistical data in the speech"],1,"The ebook states signposts are 'indicators/key words that alert the audience to a change in topic, a tangential explanation, an example, or a conclusion.'","Ebook","Other options: Signposts are structural guides, not vehicles for personal opinion. · Signposts are not limited to the end of presentations. · Statistics are not the focus of signposts — topic transitions are."],
[1,"According to the ebook, internal summaries serve to remind the listeners of what has been discussed, while foreshadowing does what?",["Introduces unrelated topics","Emphasizes what is coming next","Provides statistical backing","Ends the presentation"],1,"The ebook states: 'Internal summaries remind the listeners what has been discussed. Foreshadowing is emphasizing what is coming next.'","Ebook","Other options: Foreshadowing previews the future, not unrelated topics. · Statistics are a separate emphasis tool (sign-based argument). · Closing or ending is a distinct stage, not foreshadowing."],
[1,"The ebook lists ten tips for improving verbal communication. Which tip involves making an effort to remember someone's name and repeating it back occasionally?",["Tip 5: Be a good listener","Tip 9: Make Eye Contact","Tip 10: Use Their Name in Conversation","Tip 3: Speak with confidence"],2,"Tip 10 in the ebook states: 'An important factor to remember while having an active and effective conversation is making an effort to remember someone's name and repeating it back to them occasionally in conversation.'","Ebook","Other options: Good listener (Tip 5) is about processing information, not using names. · Eye Contact (Tip 9) relates to visual engagement. · Speaking with confidence (Tip 3) covers voice and body language, not name use."],
[1,"Understanding your audience in business communication involves four key considerations. Which of the following is NOT one of them?",["Research","Specificity","Need","Persuasion"],3,"The four audience understanding keys are: Research, Specificity, Need, and Strategy. Persuasion is not one of them.","Ebook","Other options: Research involves reviewing existing data about the audience. · Specificity means identifying the target group. · Strategy is the 4th key — Persuasion is not listed as an audience analysis step."],
[1,"The ebook defines perception as 'the processing, interpreting, selecting and organizing of information.' It is equated with what for most practical purposes?",["Objective truth","Reality","Memory","Intelligence"],1,"The ebook explicitly states: 'Perception is the processing, interpreting, selecting and organizing of information. It is equated with reality for most practical purposes.'","Ebook","Other options: Objective truth differs from perception — perception is subjective. · Memory involves storage and recall, not the definition of perception. · Intelligence is cognitive ability, separate from the perception definition."],
[1,"The ebook states that effective business writing follows 9 principles. Which principle is described as 'concise and exact use of words'?",["Clarity","Brevity","Communication","Emphasis"],1,"The ebook states: 'Brevity: Concise and exact use of words in writing or speech should be the priority before all kinds of business communication.'","Ebook","Other options: Clarity is about making communication easy to understand. · Communication refers to the social purpose of language. · Emphasis refers to placing important words in prominent positions."],
[1,"According to the ebook's principles of written communication, which principle states that 'the art of writing depends a good deal on putting the strongest words in the most important places'?",["Brevity","Clarity","Emphasis","Sophistication and Simplicity"],2,"The ebook states under Emphasis: 'The art of writing depends a good deal on putting the strongest words in the most important places.'","Ebook","Other options: Brevity is about conciseness, not word placement. · Clarity is about ease of understanding overall. · Sophistication and Simplicity is about avoiding exaggeration."],
[1,"The ebook describes 'Sound and Rhythm' as one of the written communication principles. What role do they play?",["Define legal requirements for business documents","Act as cherry on the cake — beautify writing and make it interesting","Replace the need for grammatical accuracy","Determine the length of documents"],1,"The ebook states about Sound and Rhythm: 'They help in beautifying and decorating writing piece and making it interesting to read.'","Ebook","Other options: Sound and Rhythm is an aesthetic principle, not a legal one. · Grammar accuracy is a separate requirement. · Document length is governed by brevity and scope, not rhythm."],
[1,"According to the ebook, body language may be defined as passive, assertive, or which of the following?",["Reactive","Aggressive","Submissive","Dominant"],1,"The ebook states: 'Body language may be defined as passive, assertive, or aggressive.'","Ebook","Other options: Reactive and Submissive are not the categories listed in the ebook. · Dominant is similar to aggressive but the ebook specifically uses the term 'aggressive'. · The three categories are passive, assertive, and aggressive — know all three."],
[1,"The ebook identifies individual differences in perception as being caused by which of the following?",["Job title and salary","Physical characteristics, psychological state, cultural background","Years of education alone","Geographic location only"],1,"The ebook states: 'Physical characteristics, psychological state, cultural background etc. can play very important role in individual differences.'","Ebook","Other options: Job title and salary are organizational factors, not perception determinants in the ebook. · Education is not separately listed as a perception determinant. · Geographic location is part of cultural background, not listed independently."],
[1,"The ebook lists effective business writing tips. Which tip advises using 'three Fs' for tone?",["Fast, Focused, Factual","Formal, Friendly, Familiar","Free, Frank, Fluid","Fair, Firm, Flexible"],1,"The ebook states: 'There are three Fs when it comes to tone: Formal, Friendly and Familiar. Reports tend to be formal while emails are often friendly.'","Ebook","Other options: Reports = Formal; Emails = Friendly; close colleagues = Familiar. · 'Fast, Focused, Factual' sounds plausible but is not from the ebook. · Familiar tone is only appropriate with colleagues you know really well."],
[2,"Business writing is defined in the ebook as a professional communication tool used by corporations to communicate with which audiences?",["Only internal audience","Only external audience","Either internal or external audience","Only government bodies"],2,"The ebook states: 'Business writing is a professional communication tool used by corporations to communicate with either an internal or external audience.'","Ebook","Other options: Business writing covers BOTH internal and external audiences, not just one. · Government bodies are a type of external audience, not a separate category. · The key word in the definition is 'either' — covering both options."],
[2,"Which of the following is NOT listed as a purpose of business writing in the ebook?",["To convey information","To direct action","To entertain readers","To influence someone to take action"],2,"The ebook lists business writing purposes as: convey information, deliver news, direct action, explain or justify, and influence someone to take action. Entertainment is not listed.","Ebook","Other options: To convey information, deliver news, direct action, explain/justify, and influence — these are the 5 purposes. · Entertainment is a purpose of creative writing, not business writing. · To explain or justify is often overlooked as a purpose — know all 5."],
[2,"The ebook lists 8 characteristics of effective business writing. Which characteristic advises choosing 'decided' rather than 'made the decision'?",["Use of everyday words","Write tight","Use contractions","Be to the point"],1,"The ebook states under characteristic 6: 'Tight writing. Choosing the word \"decided\" rather than \"made the decision\" makes reading easier for the audience.'","Ebook","Other options: 'To the point' means stating your reason upfront, not word compactness. · 'Everyday words' means choosing simpler vocabulary (e.g., 'about' vs. 'concerning'). · Write tight (characteristic 6) is specifically about eliminating wordy phrases."],
[2,"Business writing preparation involves three fundamental preliminary considerations. Which of the following correctly lists all three?",["Objective, Audience, Tone","Objective, Readers, Scope","Purpose, Format, Length","Topic, Style, Deadline"],1,"The ebook states: 'You must establish a document's objective, identify its readers and determine its scope. All other steps in the writing process follow and develop from these determinations.'","Ebook","Other options: Tone is important but not one of the 3 preliminary considerations. · Format and Length come after preparation — not part of the 3 fundamentals. · Deadline is a project management concept, not a preparation fundamental in the ebook."],
[2,"The ebook describes the five-step business writing process. What is the correct order?",["Research → Organize → Draft → Revise → Proofread","Preparation → Research → Organizing → Drafting → Review and Revision","Draft → Research → Organize → Edit → Publish","Plan → Write → Edit → Review → Submit"],1,"The ebook lists the business writing steps as: Preparation, Research and Investigation, Organizing, Drafting, and Review and Revision.","Ebook","Other options: Proofreading comes after revision and is a separate final step. · Publishing is not named in the 5-step process. · Preparation (not Planning) is the first step; Drafting (not Writing) is step 4."],
[2,"According to the ebook, plagiarism includes which of the following acts?",["Paraphrasing with citation","Summarizing with credit to source","Representing another's work as your own, including patch writing","Using external data in a business report"],2,"The ebook states: 'Plagiarism is representing another's work as your own. Patch writing, or the cut-and-paste insertion of fragments without crediting sources, is plagiarism.'","Ebook","Other options: Paraphrasing WITH citation is acceptable — it is not plagiarism. · Summarizing WITH credit is also acceptable. · Patch writing (cut-and-paste without credit) is specifically identified as plagiarism."],
[2,"The ebook states that 'information literacy is an essential skill set in the process of writing' and relates it to which broader concern?",["Speed of writing","Business and industry reputation and trust","Memorization of facts","Use of visual aids"],1,"The ebook states: 'Information literacy is an essential skill set in the process of writing. Business and industry rely on reputation and trust in order to maintain healthy relationships.'","Ebook","Other options: Speed of writing is not connected to information literacy in the ebook. · Memorization is a learning technique, not an information literacy concern. · Visual aids are an emphasis strategy, not related to information literacy directly."],
[2,"The organizing principle concept in the ebook is described as a core assumption that allows all other objects to be located. It is compared to what?",["A filing cabinet","A central reference point like a conceptual framework","An executive summary","A table of contents"],1,"The ebook states: 'An organizing principle is a core assumption from which everything else by proximity can derive a classification or a value. It is like a central reference point that allows all other objects to be located, often used in a conceptual framework.'","Ebook","Other options: A filing cabinet is a physical analogy not used in the ebook. · Executive summary is a document section, not an organizing principle. · Table of contents is a navigation tool, not the definition of organizing principle."],
[2,"The 'Scalar Principle' in the ebook's organizing principles is also known as which of the following?",["Unity of Command","Chain of Command","Span of Control","Delegation of Authority"],1,"The ebook states: 'Scalar Principle: Also known as the \"chain of command\", it is unbroken line of authority from the top level to the bottom of an organization.'","Ebook","Other options: Unity of Command says each employee should have ONE boss — different from scalar. · Span of Control is a numerical limit on subordinates per manager. · Delegation of Authority is about assigning authority at all management levels."],
[2,"According to the ebook, the 'Unity of Command' principle suggests that an employee should have how many bosses?",["Two","As many as required","One and only one","Three or fewer"],2,"The ebook states: 'Unity of Command: It suggests that an employee should have one and only one boss. Directions from several superiors may result in confusion, chaos, conflict.'","Ebook","Other options: Multiple bosses cause confusion and conflict per the ebook. · The principle is absolute: ONE boss, not 'as many as required'. · Unity of Direction is a different principle about group goals — not the same as Unity of Command."],
[2,"Revision in the writing process is defined in the ebook as the process by which you look over your writing again in order to do what?",["Submit for final publication","Correct or improve your message","Add more content","Delete entire sections"],1,"The ebook states: 'Revision is the process by which you look over again in order to correct or improve your message.'","Ebook","Other options: Submission comes after revision, not as part of it. · Adding content might be a result of revision, but the definition focuses on correction/improvement. · Deleting entire sections may happen but is not the definition of revision."],
[2,"Proofreading, according to the ebook, includes reviewing for which of the following?",["New research and additional arguments","Grammar, correct use of language, and format","Audience analysis and scope","Document structure and organizing principles"],1,"The ebook states: 'Proofreading includes reviewing for grammar, correct use of language, and format. It's the final touches needed to ensure a business document is correct, consistent, and professional.'","Ebook","Other options: Research belongs to the investigation step, not proofreading. · Audience analysis is a preparation activity, not proofreading. · Organizing principles are addressed in the organizing step, before drafting."],
[2,"In design evaluation, 'framing' is described in the ebook as referring to which of the following?",["The choice of font style","How information is presented, including margins, line justifications, and template expectations","Paragraph length and spacing","The visual graphics used"],1,"The ebook states: 'Framing includes all the presentation styles used. It refers to how information is presented, including margins, line justifications, and template expectations.'","Ebook","Other options: Font style is a typeface decision, separate from framing. · Paragraph structure is addressed separately under 'Paragraphs' in design evaluation. · Visual graphics are a different design element — not the definition of framing."],
[2,"The ebook identifies three sources of feedback in the writing process. Which of the following is NOT one of them?",["Peer feedback","Conferencing","Written comments","Algorithmic review"],3,"The three sources of feedback listed in the ebook are: Peer feedback, Conferencing, and Written comments. Algorithmic review is not mentioned.","Ebook","Other options: Peer feedback allows students to use peers' ideas to redraft writing. · Conferencing facilitates collaborative reflective dialogue. · Written comments can be positive or negative — the ebook lists these three only."],
[2,"The ebook lists five categories of feedback. Which category involves 'questioning for probing information'?",["Evaluative","Interpretive","Supportive","Probing"],3,"The ebook states: 'Probing: This feedback leads to questioning for probing information.'","Ebook","Other options: Evaluative feedback assigns marks/scores/grades. · Interpretive feedback provides an overall judgment by interpreting the work. · Supportive feedback contains mostly positive statements about effort."],
[2,"Quantitative research, as described in the ebook, focuses on which of the following?",["Exploring ideas and formulating theories","Testing theories and hypotheses through math and statistical analysis","Open-ended questions and subjective understanding","Summarizing, categorizing and interpreting"],1,"The ebook states quantitative research 'focuses on testing theories and hypotheses. It is analyzed through math and statistical analysis.'","Ebook","Other options: Exploring ideas and formulating theories describes qualitative research. · Open-ended questions and subjectivity characterize qualitative research. · Summarizing and interpreting are analysis methods of qualitative research."],
[2,"Qualitative research, according to the ebook, consists of which type of questions?",["Closed (multiple choice) questions","Open-ended questions","True/false questions","Ranking questions only"],1,"The ebook states qualitative research 'consists of open-ended questions' and is mainly expressed in words.","Ebook","Other options: Closed/multiple choice questions characterize quantitative research. · True/false questions are a form of closed questions — quantitative. · Ranking questions may be quantitative — qualitative uses open-ended exploration."],
[2,"According to the ebook, to paraphrase is to do which of the following?",["Copy the original text verbatim","Rewrite the information in your own words","Summarize only the main points","Remove all citations"],1,"The ebook states: 'To paraphrase is to rewrite the information in your own words. Relate the main point, but make sure not to copy the original.'","Ebook","Other options: Verbatim copying is plagiarism, not paraphrasing. · Summarizing reduces content to its most basic point — different from paraphrasing. · Paraphrasing still requires credit to the original source."],
[2,"Which characteristic of business writing advises using 'active rather than passive verbs'?",["Because it adds formality","Because it allows the reader to comprehend quickly and understand more completely","Because it increases word count","Because it avoids legal issues"],1,"The ebook states: 'Use of active rather than passive verbs. Active verbs allow the reader to comprehend quickly and understand more completely.'","Ebook","Other options: Active voice is preferred for clarity, not formality — formal writing may still use passive. · Active voice actually reduces word count, not increases it. · Legal writing often prefers passive voice — the ebook's reason is comprehension."],
[2,"The 'Understanding' category of feedback in the ebook is defined as:",["Assigning grades to work","Providing positive statements to support feelings","Understanding and explanation both taking place","Leading to questioning for deeper information"],2,"The ebook states: 'Understanding: Understanding and explanation both takes place.'","Ebook","Other options: Assigning grades is Evaluative feedback. · Positive statements describe Supportive feedback. · Leading to questioning describes Probing feedback."],
[2,"The ebook warns that font choices in business writing should be simple. What should a writer stick to?",["As many fonts as needed for visual variety","A nice, clean type style and a limited number of fonts","Bold fonts throughout for emphasis","Script fonts for a personal touch"],1,"The ebook states: 'Font choices should be simple. Stick to a nice, clean type style and limit the number of fonts you use in correspondence.'","Ebook","Other options: Multiple fonts create visual clutter — the ebook advises limiting fonts. · Bold throughout defeats the purpose of selective emphasis. · Script fonts are not business-appropriate per ebook guidance."],
[2,"The 'Span of Control' principle in organizing refers to which of the following?",["The unbroken line of authority from top to bottom","A numerical limit of subordinates to be supervised by a manager","Equal authority and responsibility at all levels","One employee, one boss"],1,"The ebook states: 'Span of Control: It is a numerical limit of subordinates to be supervised or controlled by a manager.'","Ebook","Other options: Unbroken line of authority describes the Scalar Principle (chain of command). · Equal authority and responsibility describes the Authority/Responsibility principle. · One employee, one boss describes Unity of Command."],
[2,"According to the ebook, the 'Delegation of Authority' principle states that the authority delegated should be equal to what?",["The manager's seniority","Responsibility, to enable task accomplishment","The number of subordinates","The budget allocated"],1,"The ebook states: 'The authority delegated should be equal to responsibility so as to enable each manager to accomplish the task assigned to him.'","Ebook","Other options: Seniority determines role, not the formal principle of delegation. · Number of subordinates is the Span of Control principle. · Budget is a resource allocation concept, not the delegation principle."],
[3,"The ebook advises that hiring managers typically spend approximately how many seconds reviewing each resume?",["30 seconds","6 seconds","2 minutes","1 minute"],1,"The ebook states: 'Hiring managers spend only ~6 seconds per resume' — making most-relevant information first critical.","Ebook","Other options: 30 seconds is commonly cited in pop articles but the ebook says 6 seconds. · 1–2 minutes grossly overestimates recruiter scanning time per the ebook. · The 6-second rule reinforces why relevant information must appear first."],
[3,"Which font sizes and types does the ebook recommend for a resume?",["Comic Sans, 14-16pt","Arial or Times New Roman, 10-12pt","Calibri, 8-10pt","Verdana, 12-14pt"],1,"The ebook states: 'Professional font: Arial or Times New Roman; size 10–12pt; reduce extraneous whitespace.'","Ebook","Other options: Comic Sans is not professional and is explicitly avoided. · 8-10pt is too small for a readable resume. · Calibri and Verdana may be acceptable but Arial/TNR are specifically named in the ebook."],
[3,"The ebook advises using which type of language in a resume — giving examples like 'achieved', 'earned', 'completed', 'accomplished'?",["Passive language","Technical language","Active language","Descriptive language"],2,"The ebook states: 'Active language: Power words: \"achieved,\" \"earned,\" \"completed,\" \"accomplished\".'","Ebook","Other options: Passive language (e.g., 'was responsible for') weakens resumes. · Technical language may be appropriate for specific roles but is not the general tip. · Descriptive language is subjective — the ebook emphasizes action-oriented power words."],
[3,"A cover letter is described in the ebook as having how many parts?",["4","5","6","7"],2,"The ebook lists 6 parts of a cover letter: Header, Salutation, Introduction, Body, Closing, and Signature.","Ebook","Other options: 5 parts would miss the Signature or another section. · 7 parts is an overcount. · All 6 parts are: Header → Salutation → Introduction → Body → Closing → Signature."],
[3,"What should you write in the salutation of a cover letter when the hiring manager's name is unknown?",["To Whom It May Concern","Dear Hiring Manager","Dear Sir or Madam","Dear Recruiter"],1,"The ebook states: 'Salutation: if unknown: \"Dear Hiring Manager\".'","Ebook","Other options: 'To Whom It May Concern' sounds outdated — ebook specifically says 'Dear Hiring Manager'. · 'Dear Sir or Madam' is too formal and gendered. · 'Dear Recruiter' is not the recommended phrasing in the ebook."],
[3,"What is the recommended maximum length of a cover letter according to the ebook?",["Two pages","One page","Half a page","Three paragraphs only"],1,"The ebook states cover letter formatting rule: 'Maximum one page.'","Ebook","Other options: Two pages is too long — cover letters must be concise. · Half a page may be too short to convey all 6 required parts adequately. · Three paragraphs is a style suggestion, but the hard limit is one page."],
[3,"A business proposal, according to the ebook, consists of how many steps?",["5","7","9","11"],2,"The ebook lists 9 steps: Title page, Table of contents, Executive summary, State the problem, Propose a solution, Share qualifications, Include pricing, Terms and conditions, Signature space.","Ebook","Other options: 5 steps skips several critical sections like qualifications and T&C. · 7 steps is one short of the complete list. · 11 steps overcounts — there are exactly 9 per the ebook."],
[3,"In a 9-step business proposal, the executive summary appears at which step?",["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4"],2,"Step 3 in the ebook is 'Executive summary — Why you're sending it; why your solution is best (value proposition).'","Ebook","Other options: Step 1 is the Title page. · Step 2 is the Table of contents. · Step 4 is 'State the problem or need'."],
[3,"The ebook describes 4 sales message strategies. Which strategy advises 'lead with emotion, follow with reason'?",["Strategy 1","Strategy 2","Strategy 4","Strategy 3"],3,"Strategy 4 in the ebook states: 'Lead with emotion, follow with reason — Drama/humor/novelty to grab attention → facts for credibility → call to action.'","Ebook","Other options: Strategy 1 is 'Start with your greatest benefit — use in headline'. · Strategy 2 is 'Take baby steps — one product/service at a time'. · Strategy 3 is 'Know your audience — individualize message to their wants'."],
[3,"Effective report writing in the ebook begins with which step?",["Prepare an outline","Collect required material (facts)","Determine objective — identify the problem","Edit the drafted report"],2,"The ebook's 7 steps to write an effective report begin with: 'Step 1: Determine objective — identify the problem.'","Ebook","Other options: Preparing an outline is Step 5. · Collecting required material is Step 2. · Editing the draft is Step 6."],
[3,"The ebook describes the preliminary parts of a report structure. Which of the following is part of the main text — NOT the preliminary parts?",["Table of Contents","Executive Summary","Introduction","Acknowledgements"],2,"Introduction belongs to the Main Text (Introduction → Body → Conclusions → Recommendations). Table of Contents, Executive Summary, and Acknowledgements are Preliminary Parts.","Ebook","Other options: Table of Contents is a preliminary part. · Executive Summary (Synopsis) is a preliminary part. · Acknowledgements are part of the preliminary section."],
[3,"According to the ebook, memos have a specific format that is always which of the following?",["Indirect — purpose revealed gradually","Direct — purpose clearly announced upfront","Narrative — story-based structure","Conversational — informal and personal"],1,"The ebook states memo characteristic: 'Direct Format: Always direct — purpose clearly announced upfront.'","Ebook","Other options: Indirect format builds up to the main point — memos always use DIRECT format. · Narrative structure is for reports or presentations, not memos. · Memos use a professional, formal tone — not conversational."],
[3,"The ebook lists 5 characteristics of memorandums. Which characteristic requires the subject line to be clear and concise — e.g., 'Thanksgiving weekend schedule' rather than 'holiday observance'?",["Audience Orientation","Professional, Formal Tone","Subject Emphasis","Objectivity"],2,"The ebook states: 'Subject Emphasis: Subject line = clear and concise; be specific (e.g., \"Thanksgiving weekend schedule\" not \"holiday observance\")'.","Ebook","Other options: Audience Orientation is about using terms known to all, not just management. · Professional Tone relates to organizational formality and legal standing. · Objectivity means facts only — no personal bias."],
[3,"The ebook describes 5 tips for developing business presentations. Which tip advises restricting slides to only 3 to 5 main points?",["Tell a Story","Keep it Simple","Know Your Stuff","Speak Naturally"],1,"The ebook's 'Keep it Simple' tip states: '3 to 5 main points only; simple and straightforward.'","Ebook","Other options: Tell a Story is about connecting emotionally with the audience. · Know Your Stuff is about being ready to answer questions. · Speak Naturally is about using a conversational style."],
[3,"Regarding slide use, the ebook advises presenters to do which of the following?",["Put all information on slides for reference","Display a few points; slides show images/figures; you tell the information","Read directly from slides for accuracy","Use slides as the only communication tool"],1,"The ebook states: 'Don't Put Everything on Slides — Display a few points; slides show images/figures; you tell the information.'","Ebook","Other options: Putting all information on slides makes the presenter redundant. · Reading directly from slides is poor presentation practice. · Slides support speech — they do not replace it."],
[3,"The ebook identifies 4 obstacles to overcome in a business presentation. Which obstacle is described as 'conviction of knowing more or being better than other cultures'?",["Language","Perception","Ethnocentrism","Physical Barriers"],2,"The ebook states: 'Ethnocentrism: Conviction of knowing more / being better than other cultures — leads to egoism, discrimination.'","Ebook","Other options: Language obstacle relates to jargon and slang creating barriers. · Perception obstacle relates to cultural values causing stereotypes. · Physical Barriers relate to background noise and faulty equipment."],
[3,"The ebook states that non-verbal communication is 'irreversible.' What does this mean?",["It can be corrected with follow-up words","Once expressed, it cannot be taken back","It has no lasting impact","It only affects visual communication"],1,"The ebook states: 'Non-verbal communication is irreversible — once expressed, cannot be taken back.'","Ebook","Other options: Words can clarify non-verbal signals but the original non-verbal impression persists. · Non-verbal signals leave strong lasting impressions. · Non-verbal includes body language, tone, gestures — not just visual elements."],
[3,"The ebook lists 8 organizing principles for documents. Which principle is described as 'First → Next → Now → Future'?",["Comparison","Chronology","Cause and Effect","Classification"],1,"The ebook describes Chronology as 'First → Next → Now → Future' — presenting information in time order.","Ebook","Other options: Comparison contrasts two products or arguments. · Cause and Effect establishes relationship between two events. · Classification establishes categories for organizing information."],
[3,"The 'Elimination' organizing principle in the ebook describes which approach?",["Showing contrast between items","Outlining all possibilities and eliminating them one by one","Using vivid examples to support main points","Grouping information into categories"],1,"The ebook states: 'Elimination: Outlines all possibilities and eliminates' — a process of narrowing down by ruling out options.","Ebook","Other options: Showing contrast is the Contrast principle. · Vivid examples describe the Example principle. · Grouping into categories is the Classification/Categorical principle."],
[3,"The ebook provides 9 resume writing tips. Which tip specifically advises to 'call attention to achievements' by highlighting the top 3-4 per role with measurable metrics?",["Tip 2","Tip 5","Tip 6","Tip 8"],2,"Tip 6 in the ebook states: 'Call attention to achievements: Top 3–4 achievements per role; include measurable metrics.'","Ebook","Other options: Tip 2 is 'Review industry examples — simple, brief, includes numbers'. · Tip 5 is 'Active language — power words like achieved, earned'. · Tip 8 is 'Appropriate margins — 1-inch margins; single spacing'."],
[3,"The spatial (space) organizing principle is described in the ebook as dealing with which of the following?",["Chronological order of events","Parts of something and how they fit the whole","Differences between two items","Categories of information"],1,"The ebook states the Spatial principle deals with 'Parts of something and how they fit the whole.'","Ebook","Other options: Chronological order is the Chronology principle. · Differences between items is the Contrast principle. · Categories is the Classification principle."],
[3,"The cover letter's body section, according to the ebook, should do which of the following?",["List job qualifications only","State why interested AND why an excellent candidate with specific examples","Summarize the entire resume","Request a specific salary"],1,"The ebook states: 'Body: Why interested + why excellent candidate; specific examples (show, don't just tell).'","Ebook","Other options: Listing qualifications alone is insufficient — the body must show fit with examples. · Summarizing the resume repeats information — the body should add new value. · Salary requests are inappropriate in a cover letter body per standard practice."],
[3,"The ebook advises that in a business proposal, the Terms and Conditions section (Step 8) typically covers which elements?",["Company history and awards","Timeline, pricing, payment schedules","Problem statement and solutions","Table of contents and index"],1,"The ebook states Step 8: 'Terms and conditions — Timeline, pricing, payment schedules.'","Ebook","Other options: Company history and awards are in Step 6: Share qualifications. · Problem and solution are Steps 4 and 5 respectively. · Table of contents and index are document navigation elements, not T&C."],
[3,"According to the ebook, the memo characteristic 'Audience Orientation' means using terms that are known to whom?",["Only to senior management","Only to external stakeholders","All recipients — not just management","Only to the subject matter experts"],2,"The ebook states: 'Audience Orientation: Always consider audience needs; use terms known to all — not just management.'","Ebook","Other options: Writing only for management alienates other readers of the memo. · External stakeholders receive different communications — memos are internal. · Subject matter experts need technical terms but memos should be accessible to all."],
[4,"The ebook defines presentation as a process of showing or presenting a work/project in front of an audience, typically a demonstration, introduction, lecture or speech. What are 4 listed purposes it can serve?",["Entertain, sell, debate, announce","Inform, persuade, inspire, motivate (among others)","Train, evaluate, promote, retire","Advertise, negotiate, mediate, arbitrate"],1,"The ebook states presentation is 'meant to inform, persuade, inspire, motivate, or to build good will or to present a new idea or product.'","Ebook","Other options: Entertainment is not listed as a presentation purpose in the ebook. · Debate and negotiation are separate business communication skills. · Training maps to 'To Educate' or 'To Instruct' purposes."],
[4,"The ebook lists 7 purposes of presentation. Which purpose is specifically described as involving 'panel settings where facts are presented and causes are generated'?",["To Inform","To Inspire","To Solve Problem","To Decide"],2,"The ebook states for 'To Solve Problem': 'A panel setting or other meeting where the problem is identified, the facts of the problem are presented and a list of causes is generated.'","Ebook","Other options: To Inform provides clear information — no panel or problem-solving format. · To Inspire creates reason for change — TED Talk style. · To Decide persuades audience to carry out requests interactively."],
[4,"The ebook distinguishes between 'To Educate' and 'To Instruct' presentations. Which statement correctly contrasts them?",["Educate = orders/directions; Instruct = knowledge/explanation","Educate = knowledge-focused; Instruct = directions/orders-focused","Both are identical in the ebook","Educate = TED Talk style; Instruct = panel setting"],1,"The ebook states: 'Instructive presentation focuses on instructions and orders, while educative presentation focuses on education and knowledge.'","Ebook","Other options: Educate and Instruct are deliberately contrasted — they are NOT the same. · TED Talk style is 'To Inspire', not educate or instruct. · Panel settings describe 'To Solve Problem', not instruct."],
[4,"The Freeform presentation style, according to the ebook, is used by speakers who have which characteristic?",["Short preparation time and rely on slides","Thorough knowledge with talking points memorized and no notes needed","Enthusiasm and motivational energy","Forms connections with the audience"],1,"The ebook states: 'Freeform style is used by those who have a thorough knowledge of what your company does and you have your talking points memorized, so they don't have to prepare or take notes.'","Ebook","Other options: Slides and short prep time describe Visual style. · Enthusiasm and motivational energy describe Coach style. · Forming connections describes Connector style."],
[4,"When preparing a speech to inform, the ebook advises 'Reciprocity' in the context of presentation. What does this mean?",["Getting something in return for your speech","Mutual exchange and interdependence — give first without requiring return","Repeating key points multiple times","Matching the audience's energy level"],1,"The ebook states: 'Reciprocity is a relationship of mutual exchange and interdependence. This leads to inter-dependence or the dependence of the conversational partners on one another.'","Ebook","Other options: Reciprocity in this context is about giving without expectation, not transactional exchange. · Repeating key points is the Repetition emphasis strategy from Module 1. · Matching audience energy is a delivery technique, not the definition of reciprocity."],
[4,"According to Cialdini's principles of persuasion (as listed in the ebook), the Scarcity principle works best with which type of language?",["Gain language — what you will achieve","Loss language — what you will lose out on","Technical language — detailed specifications","Emotional language — feelings and stories"],1,"The ebook states: 'If you focus more on loss language, or language that demonstrates what you will lose out on rather than gain, your message becomes more powerful.'","Ebook","Other options: Gain language is less powerful than loss language for scarcity per the ebook. · Technical language applies to Authority, not Scarcity. · Emotional language relates to Liking, not specifically to Scarcity."],
[4,"The Commitment/Consistency persuasion principle in the ebook advises getting commitments that are voluntary, active, and ideally in what form?",["Verbal only","Anonymous","In writing","Through a third party"],2,"The ebook states: 'When seeking to influence using the consistency principle, look for voluntary, active, and public commitments and ideally gets those commitments in writing.'","Ebook","Other options: Verbal commitments are weaker — written commitments create stronger consistency. · Anonymous commitments defeat the 'public' requirement. · Third-party commitments are indirect — the principle requires personal commitment."],
[4,"The Social Proof/Consensus persuasion principle states that people look to the actions of others especially when they are in what state?",["Highly confident","Uncertain","In a leadership role","Alone"],1,"The ebook states: 'People will look to the actions and behaviors of others to determine their own, especially when they are uncertain.'","Ebook","Other options: Confident people are less likely to need social proof. · Leadership roles may still use social proof but uncertainty is the key trigger. · Social proof is most powerful in group settings, not when alone."],
[4,"In the GASCAPT argument strategies, what does the 'A' in Analogy signify?",["Two situations alike in observable ways will tend to be alike in other ways","A credible source indicates what is probably true","Statistical facts indicate meaning like a stop sign","An accepted or proper truth"],0,"Analogy: 'Two situations, things or ideas are alike in observable ways and will tend to be alike in many other ways. Example: Alcohol is a drug. So is tobacco.'","Ebook","Other options: Credible source = Authority (second 'A' in GASCAPT). · Statistical facts like a stop sign = Sign (S in GASCAPT). · Accepted truth = Principle (P in GASCAPT)."],
[4,"The seventh and final step in building a strong argument, according to the ebook, is to:",["Collect evidence from multiple sources","Consider key objections","Craft your argument with claim and evidence","Confirm your main point by restating the claim in a new way"],3,"Step 7: 'Confirm your main point: Wrap up your argument by stating your claim in a new way, connecting it to real life and to the future.'","Ebook","Other options: Collecting evidence is Step 4. · Considering objections is Step 5. · Crafting your argument is Step 6."],
[4,"An elevator pitch, as defined in the ebook, should last no longer than how many seconds?",["60 to 90 seconds","10 to 15 seconds","20 to 30 seconds","45 to 60 seconds"],2,"The ebook states: 'A good elevator pitch should last no longer than a short elevator ride of 20 to 30 seconds, hence the name.'","Ebook","Other options: 60-90 seconds is too long — it should fit an elevator ride. · 10-15 seconds is too brief to communicate a proposition effectively. · 45-60 seconds doubles the recommended time."],
[4,"The second stage of a five-stage telephone conversation, according to the ebook, is called 'Feed Forward'. What happens during this stage?",["Both parties say goodbye","Parties exchange roles in conversation","Purpose and tone of conversation are established; permission given to continue","Business of the conversation is concluded"],2,"The ebook states for Feed Forward: 'Purpose and tone of conversation are established. Permission is given to continue (or not).'","Ebook","Other options: Goodbye is the Closing stage (Stage 5). · Exchanging roles describes the Business stage (Stage 3). · Concluding business describes the Feedback stage (Stage 4)."],
[4,"VoIP, as defined in the ebook, is also called what?",["Digital telephony","IP telephony","Wireless communication","Cloud calling"],1,"The ebook states: 'Voice over Internet Protocol (VoIP), also called IP telephony.'","Ebook","Other options: Digital telephony is a broader term not used in the ebook's specific definition. · Wireless communication describes radio-based systems, not VoIP specifically. · Cloud calling is a modern term not used in the ebook's definition."],
[4,"A sound bite is defined in the ebook as a brief excerpt from a text or performance meant to do which of the following?",["Replace a full presentation","Capture the interest and attention of an audience","Provide statistical evidence","Serve as the conclusion of a speech"],1,"The ebook states: 'Sound bite is a brief excerpt from a text or performance (ranging from a single word to a sentence or two) that is meant to capture the interest and attention of an audience.'","Ebook","Other options: Sound bites support presentations — they do not replace them. · Sound bites are memorable excerpts, not statistical data. · Sound bites can appear anywhere in a presentation, not just conclusions."],
[4,"The ebook clarifies that a roast, unlike a toast, is designed to honor someone by allowing them to demonstrate what?",["Their public speaking skills","They can take a joke at their own expense gracefully","Their knowledge of the subject","Their authority over subordinates"],1,"The ebook states: 'A roast is designed to honor someone in a position of power or influence by allowing them to demonstrate they can take a joke at their own expense gracefully.'","Ebook","Other options: Public speaking skills are not the focus of a roast. · Knowledge demonstration is for instructional presentations. · Roasts specifically target people in POWER — it's about graceful humor, not authority."],
[4,"Formal meetings, according to the ebook, require which of the following?",["No written record","A notice or invitation and written record (minutes)","Only video conferencing format","At least 10 participants"],1,"The ebook states: 'Formal meeting has set rules for convening and conducting it, with a written record of its proceedings (minutes of meeting). It requires a notice or an invitation to attend it.'","Ebook","Other options: No written record describes informal meetings. · Format (face-to-face, video, teleconference) applies to both formal and informal. · There is no minimum participant count mentioned in the ebook."],
[4,"A consultative meeting is defined in the ebook as a meeting where:",["Decisions by empowered persons are announced","Information or instructions are given","Members of the group are consulted for a solution to a problem","Company financials are reviewed"],2,"The ebook states: 'Consultative meeting: Members of the group are consulted for solution of a certain problem.'","Ebook","Other options: Decisions announced = Executive meeting. · Information/instructions given = Informative meeting. · Financial review is a business activity, not a meeting type per the ebook."],
[4,"Intrapersonal communication is described in the ebook as the foundation of which broader communication ability?",["Group communication","Your communication ability with all external resources","Mass media communication","Written business communication only"],1,"The ebook states: 'It is the foundation of your communication ability with all external resources, because how you view your position in your surroundings will always affect how you interact with others.'","Ebook","Other options: Group communication builds on interpersonal skills, not purely intrapersonal. · Intrapersonal influences ALL external communication, not just mass media. · Written communication is a subset — intrapersonal underpins everything external."],
[4,"Self-regulation, the second component of developing intrapersonal communication, is associated with which qualities according to the ebook?",["Enthusiasm and creativity","Integrity, reliability and adaptability","Analytical ability and research skills","Empathy and persuasion"],1,"The ebook states: 'Those who have control over self-regulation demonstrate higher levels of integrity, reliability and adaptability.'","Ebook","Other options: Enthusiasm relates to motivation, not self-regulation specifically. · Analytical ability relates to research and critical thinking. · Empathy relates to interpersonal skills, not the definition of self-regulation."],
[4,"Active listening is defined in the ebook as practiced by doing which of the following?",["Nodding frequently during conversation","Repeating back a speaker's comment to confirm true communication","Taking notes on everything said","Making direct eye contact only"],1,"The ebook states: 'Active listening can be practiced by repeating back a speaker's comment to make sure true communication is taking place.'","Ebook","Other options: Nodding is a non-verbal cue but not the definition of active listening practice. · Taking notes aids memory but is not the active listening technique defined. · Eye contact is a non-verbal skill, separate from active listening practice."],
[4,"According to the ebook, the Authority persuasion principle works by having people follow the lead of whom?",["Peers with similar backgrounds","Credible, knowledgeable experts","Those with the loudest voices","Popular social media influencers"],1,"The ebook states: 'Authority: This is the idea that people follow the lead of credible, knowledgeable experts.'","Ebook","Other options: Peers influence through Liking or Social Proof, not Authority. · Loudness is not a determinant of authority in the ebook. · Social media influencers may overlap with authority but the ebook specifies credible experts."],
[4,"The Argument by Cause (GASCAPT) states that if two conditions always appear together, they are:",["Coincidentally related","Causally related","Analogous","Symbolic"],1,"The ebook states: 'Argument by Cause: If two conditions always appear together, they are causally related.'","Ebook","Other options: Coincidence implies no causal link — the ebook says causally related. · Analogy is a different GASCAPT strategy comparing similar situations. · Symbolic relationships belong to language theory, not argument strategy."],
[4,"The Liking principle of persuasion states that people tend to believe a person they like is what?",["More qualified","Trustworthy","More intelligent","More senior"],1,"The ebook states: 'People we like tend to have the same beliefs, interests, and language as we do. So the mind tends to believe that a person we like is trustworthy.'","Ebook","Other options: Liking relates to trust and shared values, not perceived qualification. · Intelligence is associated with Authority, not Liking. · Seniority is unrelated to Liking in the ebook."],
[5,"The ebook lists 7 goals when delivering negative news. Which goal specifically refers to avoiding legal liability or admission of guilt?",["Goal 2","Goal 3","Goal 4","Goal 5"],2,"Goal 4 in the ebook states: 'Avoid legal liability or admission of guilt or culpability.'","Ebook","Other options: Goal 2 is 'Help the receiver understand and accept the news'. · Goal 3 is 'Maintain trust and respect for the business and receiver'. · Goal 5 is 'Maintain the relationship even if the formal association is being terminated'."],
[5,"According to the ebook, the seventh goal when delivering negative news is to do which of the following?",["Reduce anxiety associated with the news","Maintain the relationship","Achieve the designated business outcome","Be clear and concise"],2,"Goal 7 in the ebook: 'Achieve the designated business outcome.'","Ebook","Other options: Reduce anxiety is Goal 6. · Maintain the relationship is Goal 5. · Be clear and concise is Goal 1."],
[5,"The crisis communication plan outlined in the ebook contains four elements. Which element prevents 'egocentric behavior and chorus talking'?",["Crisis communication team with contact information","Designated spokesperson","Meeting place/location","Media plan with procedures"],1,"The ebook states the Designated spokesperson 'prevents issues like egocentric behavior, chorus talking, indecisiveness.'","Ebook","Other options: The crisis team prevents indecisiveness — but chorus talking is specifically the spokesperson's role. · Meeting place ensures an undisturbed location, not communication discipline. · Media plan prevents rumors — separate from internal spokesperson function."],
[5,"The ebook defines ethnocentrism as the belief that one's own cultural group is:",["Most economically advanced","Innately superior to others","Most linguistically complex","The originator of all civilization"],1,"The ebook states: 'Ethnocentrism is the belief that one's own cultural group is somehow innately superior to others.'","Ebook","Other options: Economic advancement is not part of the ethnocentrism definition. · Linguistic complexity is unrelated to ethnocentrism's definition. · Historical claims of originating civilization are cultural myths, not the definition."],
[5,"Ethnocentrism is described in the ebook as 'deceptive' because of which reason?",["It is rarely noticed by outsiders","Members perceive their own behavior as logical since it works for them","It only affects corporate communication","It is a temporary phenomenon"],1,"The ebook states: 'Ethnocentrism is deceptive because members of any culture perceive their own behavior as logical, since that behavior works for them.'","Ebook","Other options: Outsider perception is not the reason for ethnocentrism being deceptive. · Ethnocentrism affects all cross-cultural communication, not just corporate. · The ebook does not describe it as temporary."],
[5,"The ebook identifies 5 factors affecting cross-cultural business communication. Which factor is described as 'the most often cited barrier'?",["Environment and Technology","Social Organization and History","Language","Nonverbal Communication"],2,"The ebook states: 'Language is one of the most often cited barriers in cross-cultural business communication.'","Ebook","Other options: Environment and Technology is a factor but not cited as the most common barrier. · Social Organization raises complex issues but Language tops the list. · Nonverbal communication is significant but Language is explicitly the most cited."],
[5,"The 'Conceptions of Authority' cross-cultural factor in the ebook refers to which of the following?",["Physical distance between offices","How each culture distributes authority and how rank affects message sending","The legal system of each country","Technology tools used by authority figures"],1,"The ebook states: 'Every culture has its own distribution of authority in their society. Thought process of the authority in a given society affect business communication since they decide the way of working and transfer of messages based on the relative status or rank of the message sender and receiver.'","Ebook","Other options: Physical distance relates to Environment, not Authority conceptions. · Legal systems are governance issues, not the communication factor described. · Technology tools are part of the Environment and Technology factor."],
[5,"The ebook distinguishes a group from a crowd by stating that a group has which characteristic that a crowd lacks?",["Large number of people","Common goals","Physical proximity","A leader"],1,"The ebook states: 'Similar to crowd, a group focuses to look at a particular interest. But dissimilar to the crowd, a group is more than just a collection of people. There are common goals to accomplish.'","Ebook","Other options: Crowds can also be large — size is not the differentiator. · Physical proximity describes crowds too — goals are the key difference. · Crowds may have informal leaders temporarily — formal common goals are what groups have."],
[5,"According to the ebook, group communication is defined as interaction and exchange of information between people who are:",["In the same geographic location only","Culturally, geographically or linguistically alike and members of a group","Senior management only","Externally facing customer service teams"],1,"The ebook states: 'Group communication is interaction and exchange of information between culturally, geographically or linguistically alike people who are a member of a group.'","Ebook","Other options: Geographic location alone is one dimension — the definition includes cultural and linguistic similarity too. · Senior management is not the limit — group communication applies at all levels. · External-facing teams are a subtype — the definition is broader."],
[5,"The ebook states that quality group communication requires which three elements?",["Speed, volume, and frequency","Sense of belonging, mutual respect, and common goals","Authority, hierarchy, and procedures","Technology, infrastructure, and bandwidth"],1,"The ebook states: 'To achieve this, the group members must possess a sense of belonging and mutual respect to each other along with common goals.'","Ebook","Other options: Speed and frequency relate to communication efficiency, not quality. · Authority and hierarchy are organizational structures, not group communication quality elements. · Technology is a tool, not a quality communication element per this ebook section."],
[5,"According to the ebook, the more a group discusses, the more it tends to do which of the following?",["Reach unanimous consensus","Polarize into two extreme opinions","Become passive and silent","Increase individual creativity"],1,"The ebook states: 'The more a group discusses, the more, it tends to polarize into two extreme opinions.'","Ebook","Other options: Unanimous consensus is the opposite of polarization. · Passivity and silence are disadvantages of individual members, not group psychology. · Individual creativity may decrease in groups due to conformity pressure."],
[5,"The ebook states the primary purpose of group communication is to do which of the following?",["Make decisions","Manage conflicts","Share information","Enhance leadership"],2,"The ebook explicitly states: 'The primary purpose of group communication is to share information.'","Ebook","Other options: Decision-making and conflict management are secondary purposes. · Leadership enhancement is an outcome, not the primary purpose. · The ebook is explicit: sharing information is primary."],
[5,"Teamwork is defined in the ebook as the collaborative effort of a group to achieve a common goal in what manner?",["Quickly and cheaply","Creatively and flexibly","Effectively and efficiently","Boldly and ambitiously"],2,"The ebook states: 'Teamwork is the collaborative effort of a group to achieve a common goal or to complete a task in the most effective and efficient way.'","Ebook","Other options: Speed and cost are not the stated terms — 'effective and efficient' is the exact phrasing. · Creativity and flexibility are benefits but not the definitional words. · Bold and ambitious are motivational qualities, not in the teamwork definition."],
[5,"Andrew Carnegie's quote about teamwork in the ebook describes it as 'the fuel that allows common people to attain' what?",["Common results","Uncommon results","Financial rewards","Managerial positions"],1,"Carnegie's quote in the ebook: 'It is the fuel that allows common people to attain uncommon results.'","Ebook","Other options: 'Common results' is the opposite of what Carnegie says. · Financial rewards are an outcome but not what the quote states. · Managerial positions are career outcomes, not the quote's focus."],
[5,"An emergent leader, according to the ebook, grows into the leadership role in which way?",["By being appointed by a senior authority","Through election by group members","Through group interaction over time and support of others","By having the highest technical skill"],2,"The ebook states: 'An emergent leader grows into the role, often out of necessity... The individual acquires emergent leadership through the support and acceptance of other people in the organization.'","Ebook","Other options: Appointment by authority = Appointed leader. · Election by group = Democratic leader. · Technical skill describes Leader-as-Technician style, not emergent leadership category."],
[5,"The laissez-faire leadership style is described in the ebook as 'live and let live.' What is the challenge of this style?",["Generating enough authority to command respect","Balancing the need for control with the need for space","Communicating complex technical information","Managing cross-cultural teams"],1,"The ebook states: 'Balancing the need for control with the need for space is the challenge of the laissez-faire leader.'","Ebook","Other options: Authority generation is an Autocratic challenge — laissez-faire has too much space, not too little. · Technical information communication describes Leader-as-Technician. · Cross-cultural management relates to Module 5's intercultural factors, not laissez-faire leadership specifically."],
[5,"The 'leader-as-coach' style in the ebook combines which roles?",["Manager and accountant","Teacher, motivator, and keeper of the goals of the group","Technician and decision-maker","Spokesperson and negotiator"],1,"The ebook states: 'A leader-as-coach combines many of the talents and skills, serving as a teacher, motivator, and keeper of the goals of the group.'","Ebook","Other options: Manager and accountant are organizational roles, not the coach definition. · Technician and decision-maker describe Leader-as-Technician. · Spokesperson relates to crisis communication, not the coach leadership style."],
[5,"The three-step process for writing routine and positive messages begins with Planning. What does the Writing step (Step 2) advise about the opening of the message?",["End with the main idea","Open by stating the request or main idea","Begin with background information only","Start with a lengthy introduction"],1,"The ebook states for Step 2 Writing: 'Open by stating the request or main idea, give necessary details in the body, close with a cordial request for specific action.'","Ebook","Other options: Main idea goes in the opening, not the end — this is the direct approach. · Background information alone is an indirect approach, contradicting positive message strategy. · Lengthy introductions reduce clarity — the ebook advises direct opening."],
[5,"The strategy for routine messages advises using which approach for positive messages?",["Indirect approach — build up to the good news","Direct approach — begin with the main idea or good news","Passive approach — let the reader infer the good news","Delayed approach — reveal at the end"],1,"The ebook states: 'Use the direct approach for positive messages. Prepare your audience for the detail that follows by beginning your positive message with the main idea or good news.'","Ebook","Other options: Indirect approach is for negative news delivery, not positive messages. · Passive approach is not listed as a message strategy. · Delayed approach contradicts the direct approach principle."],
[5,"Group norms, as defined in the ebook, are described as standards, customs, and behavioral expectations that:",["Are imposed only by management","Emerge from members as a group forms — self and group imposed","Are written into company policy","Are determined at hiring"],1,"The ebook states: 'Group norms are standards, customs, and behavioral expectations. These are self and group imposed expectations' that emerge from members as the group forms.","Ebook","Other options: Management imposes formal rules — group norms emerge organically from members. · Company policy is formal — norms are informal behavioral expectations. · Hiring sets individual expectations — norms emerge through group interaction."],
[5,"The ebook states that feedback in the writing process is the final step in communication and is defined as:",["The editing process","A receiver's response to a source","The publishing of a document","The research phase"],1,"The ebook states: 'The final step in the communication process is feedback. Feedback is defined as a receiver's response to a source, and can come in many forms.'","Ebook","Other options: Editing is part of revision — a writing process step before feedback. · Publishing is distribution — feedback follows receipt, not distribution. · Research is the second step in writing, not feedback."],
[5,"The ebook describes one disadvantage of group communication: personally related group members may get involved in personal discussions, leading to what outcome?",["Enhanced group bonding","Deviation from company goals affecting personal goals","Improved morale and creativity","Better conflict resolution"],1,"The ebook states: 'Personally related group members may get involved in personal discussion leading them to deviate from the company goals, thus affecting personal goals.'","Ebook","Other options: Personal discussions are a disadvantage, not a bonding benefit in this context. · Morale and creativity are benefits of positive group communication. · Conflict resolution is an advantage of group communication, not related to this disadvantage."]
  ]
};
